WEBVTT

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:03.929
Sites hold data that people need elsewhere:

00:00:03.929 --> 00:00:07.813
a list for finance, a file for a partner, rows to bring in.

00:00:07.813 --> 00:00:12.894
The Data Pipelines module reads data, changes it and delivers it, without code.

00:00:12.894 --> 00:00:16.360
In this video, we'll build and run a first pipeline.

00:00:20.960 --> 00:00:24.320
Data often has to leave the site or enter it:

00:00:24.320 --> 00:00:29.392
a spreadsheet of content items, a nightly file for another system, or a

00:00:29.392 --> 00:00:32.488
CSV file whose rows become content items.

00:00:32.488 --> 00:00:35.650
Done by hand, the work repeats every time.

00:00:36.400 --> 00:00:41.299
The Data Pipelines module lets an administrator design this work as a pipeline:

00:00:41.299 --> 00:00:43.086
a set of connected steps.

00:00:43.086 --> 00:00:45.968
Sources read rows from the data of the site.

00:00:45.968 --> 00:00:47.870
Transforms change the rows.

00:00:48.620 --> 00:00:53.915
Files write the rows to a CSV file, an Excel workbook, or another format.

00:00:53.915 --> 00:00:56.461
Destinations deliver the result:

00:00:56.461 --> 00:01:01.220
to the media library, by email, to a server, or as content items.

00:01:05.455 --> 00:01:08.206
Data Pipelines is a feature of the site.

00:01:08.206 --> 00:01:11.315
Let's open Tools, Features, and search for it.

00:01:11.773 --> 00:01:17.897
Enabling Data Pipelines also enables Data Pipelines Core, which provides the data sources

00:01:17.897 --> 00:01:19.638
and the file formats.

00:01:19.638 --> 00:01:24.215
The Tools menu then has a Data Pipelines section, with two pages:

00:01:24.215 --> 00:01:26.343
Pipelines, and Run History.

00:01:30.643 --> 00:01:34.871
The Pipelines page lists the pipelines of the site, and it is empty.

00:01:34.871 --> 00:01:37.843
Add Pipeline opens the form for the first one.

00:01:38.309 --> 00:01:40.078
A pipeline needs a name.

00:01:40.078 --> 00:01:42.850
Let's type Blog posts, and select Create.

00:01:42.850 --> 00:01:44.619
The designer opens next.

00:01:45.070 --> 00:01:51.642
The designer has Steps on the left, grouped as Sources, Transforms, Files and Destinations.

00:01:51.642 --> 00:01:55.707
The canvas is empty and offers to start with a source.

00:01:55.707 --> 00:02:00.450
On the right, a panel has the Pipeline, Issues and History tabs.

00:02:04.750 --> 00:02:07.007
A pipeline starts with a source.

00:02:07.007 --> 00:02:10.393
Add a data source opens the data sets you may read:

00:02:10.393 --> 00:02:15.740
the content items of each content type, the users, and the results of saved queries.

00:02:16.798 --> 00:02:19.419
Let's add the Blog Post content items.

00:02:19.419 --> 00:02:24.006
A Read a data source step appears on the canvas, and its settings open:

00:02:24.006 --> 00:02:27.818
the fields to read, filters, and a maximum number of rows.

00:02:28.279 --> 00:02:32.269
In the Data tab, Preview runs the pipeline up to this step, on

00:02:32.269 --> 00:02:33.853
a sample of the rows.

00:02:33.853 --> 00:02:37.139
The Fields tab lists the fields the step produces.

00:02:37.613 --> 00:02:41.627
To add the next step, click the output port on the right edge of the step.

00:02:41.627 --> 00:02:44.337
The list offers the steps that can receive rows.

00:02:44.337 --> 00:02:45.993
Let's choose Create a file.

00:02:46.456 --> 00:02:49.170
Create a file writes the rows to a file.

00:02:49.170 --> 00:02:53.477
Its settings hold the format, which is CSV by default, and the file

00:02:53.477 --> 00:02:55.483
name, without its extension.

00:02:55.483 --> 00:02:57.666
Let's name the file blog-posts.

00:02:58.118 --> 00:02:59.892
A file has to go somewhere.

00:02:59.892 --> 00:03:04.355
Click the output port of Create a file, and choose Save to the media library.

00:03:04.355 --> 00:03:08.818
Its settings name a folder, and whether to replace a file with the same name.

00:03:13.035 --> 00:03:15.709
The designer saves the draft as you work.

00:03:15.709 --> 00:03:19.352
The Issues tab lists what must be fixed before publishing.

00:03:19.352 --> 00:03:23.165
Publish creates the first version, which is the one runs use.

00:03:23.625 --> 00:03:25.683
Run starts the published version.

00:03:25.683 --> 00:03:29.588
The run is queued and executes in the background, with the access of

00:03:29.588 --> 00:03:31.805
the user who published the pipeline.

00:03:32.263 --> 00:03:34.500
The History tab lists the runs.

00:03:34.500 --> 00:03:38.853
With a run selected, each step on the canvas shows its status, and

00:03:38.853 --> 00:03:41.573
the number of rows or files it handled.

00:03:42.033 --> 00:03:44.109
Open the run shows its page:

00:03:44.109 --> 00:03:48.080
each step with its status and its rows in and out, what the

00:03:48.080 --> 00:03:50.095
run delivered, and its log.

00:03:50.095 --> 00:03:53.333
Here, it saved a CSV file to the media library.

00:03:53.784 --> 00:03:58.595
The Run History page lists the runs, the most recent first, with their

00:03:58.595 --> 00:04:02.013
status, version, duration, and who started them.

00:04:02.013 --> 00:04:04.924
A link shows the runs of every pipeline.

00:04:05.381 --> 00:04:10.678
On the Pipelines page, the pipeline shows its version and the status of its last run.

00:04:10.678 --> 00:04:15.101
Its Actions menu can run it again, open its Run history, or delete it.

00:04:19.301 --> 00:04:22.420
Pipelines do more than this first export:

00:04:22.420 --> 00:04:29.389
transforms filter, sort, group, join and calculate, and destinations include download links, email,

00:04:29.389 --> 00:04:32.110
FTP and SFTP servers, and web APIs.

00:04:32.110 --> 00:04:35.163
Some steps need another feature enabled.

00:04:35.163 --> 00:04:38.481
A workflow can run a pipeline on a schedule.

00:04:39.231 --> 00:04:41.705
Three permissions control pipelines:

00:04:41.705 --> 00:04:46.889
Manage data pipelines, Run data pipelines, and View data pipelines and their runs.

00:04:46.889 --> 00:04:49.422
The Administrator role has all three.

00:04:49.422 --> 00:04:54.724
Manage data pipelines is security critical, since a published pipeline runs with the

00:04:54.724 --> 00:04:56.491
access of its publisher.

00:04:58.491 --> 00:05:02.805
Data Pipelines reads the data of your site, changes it and delivers it,

00:05:02.805 --> 00:05:04.934
and keeps a history of its runs.

00:05:04.934 --> 00:05:06.391
Thanks for watching.

